course: dictionary
lesson: 6642
title: Texture words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word like nubbly tells you how something feels when you touch it. Its root is nub, a small lump, so nubbly means covered with small lumps or bumps. Wool, tree bark and some animal skins can all be nubbly.
explain: Look at the context: «Nice but nubbly.» The word but is a clue. It sets two ideas against each other, so nubbly is probably not a compliment. The dictionary says nubbly means lumpy, so you can swap lumpy into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Writers choose playful words like nubbly to help us picture a texture. Rudyard Kipling uses funny describing words like this in his Just So Stories. In science lessons, we describe materials with words like smooth, rough, bumpy and nubbly.
fact: Nubbly is an adjective, so it describes a noun, such as a nubbly jumper.
fact: Nubbly means covered with small lumps or bumps, and lumpy and knobbly to touch.
fact: Nubbly comes from «nub», a small lump.
fact: «Nubbly» is a rare word, so writers often use it to make a description playful and vivid.
fact: In science, bumpy and nubbly both describe a rough surface, but nubbly suggests many small lumps.
exercise: «Her coat was made of nubbly wool.» What does nubbly mean? | Bright red | *Covered with small lumps | Smooth and shiny | Wet and cold
exercise: Find the one word in «Nice but nubbly» that sets the two ideas against each other. | Nubbly | Nice | *But
exercise: Which word would a scientist use to describe a material with small bumps all over it? | *Bumpy | Liquid | Shiny | Glassy
test: The Stute Fish says Man is «nice but nubbly». What does nubbly mean here? | Bright and shiny | *Lumpy and knobbly to touch | Very tall | Very quick
test: What is the word nubbly built from? | Nubble, a bright light | *Nub, a small lump | Nut, a hard seed | Knob, a large door handle
exam: Which sentence uses nubbly correctly? | The pond was nubbly, as flat and calm as glass | The song was nubbly and very quick | *The bark on the old oak tree was nubbly under my fingers | He ran nubbly across the field
